doption","Aging-cohort replacement raises 65+ internet adoption as more-connected cohorts enter age 65","Broadband definition includes cellular data, satellite, fixed wireless, and other non-dial-up service","Adoption gains slow as the remaining offline 65+ population is more selected by age, disability, income, and rurality","Affordable Connectivity Program expiration creates affordability drag for low-income older households","ACS first-print sampling and weighting noise can move a state age subgroup by more than the national series"],"sourceContext":["https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2025.B28005?g=040XX00US08&q=B28005","https://api.census.gov/data/2024/acs/acs1/groups/B28005.html","https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2024.B28005?q=B28005","https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2024.B28005?g=040XX00US06&q=Telephone%2C+Computer%2C+and+Internet+Access","https://censusreporter.org/profiles/04000US08-colorado/","https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/news/data-releases/2024/release-schedule.html","https://www.census.gov/data/what-is-data-census-gov/upcoming-releases.html"],"runAt":"2026-07-21T09:20:34Z","reasoning":[{"kind":"heading","text":"Forecast for Colorado 2025 ACS 65+ Broadband Subscription Share"},{"kind":"text","text":"Framing and exact resolver: the target is Census ACS Detailed Table B28005, universe population in households, Colorado, 2025 ACS 1-year first print. The numerator is B28005_017E, 65 years and over, has a computer, with a broadband Internet subscription; the denominator is B28005_014E, total 65 years and over. The variant is ACS 1-Year Estimates Detailed Tables, not 5-year, supplemental, PUMS, or later revised tables."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Inspect Census API variable metadata for 2024 ACS 1-year B28005","result":"Fetched variable definitions: B28005 has 78 variables; B28005_014E is total 65 years and over; B28005_017E is 65 years and over with a computer and a broadband Internet subscription; B28005_001E is the total table population count."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Inspect public Census data.census.gov 2021 and 2022 ACS 1-year B28005 United States rows from published public Census-derived artifacts","result":"Fetched 2021 ACS 1-year United States B28005 counts: 65 years and over total 55,086,561 and 65+ with broadband subscription 43,757,048, computed share 79.4 percent. Fetched 2022 ACS 1-year counts: 65 years and over total 56,337,585 and 65+ with broadband subscription 45,989,221, computed share 81.6 percent."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Inspect public Census data.census.gov 2023 and 2024 ACS 1-year B28005 United States rows from published public Census-derived artifacts","result":"Fetched 2023 ACS 1-year United States B28005 counts: 65 years and over total 57,740,580 and 65+ with broadband subscription 48,245,700, computed share 83.5 percent. Fetched 2024 ACS 1-year counts: 65 years and over total 59,125,675 and 65+ with broadband subscription 50,144,228, computed share 84.8 percent."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Inspect Census data.census.gov 2024 ACS 1-year B28005 California row as high-adoption state benchmark","result":"Fetched California 2024 ACS 1-year B28005 counts: total population in households 38,537,958; 65 years and over total 6,333,225; 65+ with broadband subscription 5,786,620; computed share 91.4 percent."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Inspect Census-derived 2024 ACS 1-year Colorado profile and ACS release schedule pages","result":"Fetched Colorado 2024 ACS 1-year profile measures: population 5,957,494; median household income 97,113 dollars; poverty 9.6 percent; senior poverty 9 percent; households 2,479,892. Fetched 2024 ACS release schedule showing 1-Year Data Release with Detailed Tables and Summary File on September 11, 2025; current Census pages did not expose a separate 2025 ACS schedule confirming 2026-09-10, so I keep the ledger date and flag that release-calendar discrepancy rather than silently changing the target."},{"kind":"text","text":"Base rate/reference class: the best same-series reference class I could verify is the U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 age-65-plus series, which rose from 79.4 percent in 2021 to 84.8 percent in 2024. Colorado should sit above that national level because its 2024 median household income was 97,113 dollars and total poverty was 9.6 percent, while California's 2024 same-table high-adoption state benchmark was 91.4 percent."},{"kind":"text","text":"Level, momentum, one-off, and mechanism: level starts from a national 2025 prior near 86.0 percent, then adds a Colorado premium for higher income, lower poverty, and educated/urban Front Range composition, while holding below California's 91.4 percent benchmark because Colorado has more rural mountain and plains geography. Momentum remains positive from cohort replacement and cellular-data-plan inclusion. BEAD deployments are unlikely to move 2025 ACS collection much, and ACP expiration is a small downside drag."},{"kind":"math","text":"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is the 2024 same-series U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 share of 84.8 percent. Historical sample is the same-series annual changes 2021-2022: 81.6 - 79.4 = +2.2, 2022-2023: 83.5 - 81.6 = +1.9, and 2023-2024: 84.8 - 83.5 = +1.3 percentage points, giving sigma = sqrt((2.2^2 + 1.9^2 + 1.3^2) / 3) = 1.8 percentage points as an annual-change RMS uncertainty proxy. National mechanism update adds +1.2 points to 86.0 for cohort replacement and continued subscription diffusion net of affordability drag; Colorado adjustment adds +1.7 points for income/poverty/composition but stays below the 91.4 California benchmark, yielding point 87.7. The 80% half-width is about 1.28*sigma = 1.28*1.8 = 2.3, widened slightly to 2.4 for state-level ACS sampling and first-print table noise, so bounds are 87.7 - 2.4 = 85.3 and 87.7 + 2.4 = 90.1."},{"kind":"text","text":"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected jump in older-adult cellular-data-plan reporting or high-income retiree adoption in Colorado, which would land above the interval if the 2025 first print exceeds 90.1 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, rural non-adoption, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 85.3 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a large ACS sampling/weighting move or a genuine state-level adoption break."},{"kind":"text","text":"Prior run update: I found only a prompt stub for this Colorado target in local records, not a completed prior same-target forecast. Existing catalog point estimates and intervals were not used as evidence; the only local artifacts used were public Census-derived history and target metadata."},{"kind":"forecast","point":87.7,"ciLow":85.3,"ciHigh":90.1}]}
