ger-than-expected jump in older-adult cellular-data-plan reporting or high-income retiree adoption in Colorado, which would land above the interval if the 2025 first print exceeds 90.1 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, rural non-adoption, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 85.3 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a large ACS sampling/weighting move or a genuine state-level adoption break."},{"kind":"text","text":"Prior run update: I found only a prompt stub for this Colorado target in local records, not a completed prior same-target forecast. Existing catalog point estimates and intervals were not used as evidence; the only local artifacts used were public Census-derived history and target metadata."},{"kind":"forecast","point":87.7,"ciLow":85.3,"ciHigh":90.1}]}
