page in the indexed official pages, so the chosen 2026-09-10 date should be treated as tied to the expected ACS 1-year September first-release slot and rechecked against any later Census ACS schedule notice.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent ACS computer-and-internet measures show most U.S. households already have broadband, but the 65+ population remains below the all-household average because disability, fixed-income, rurality, and cohort effects are concentrated among older adults. The 2023 ACS 5-year same-table United States benchmark was 82.6 percent, while 2024 California was 91.4 percent, so a 2025 U.S. 1-year value in the mid-80s is a conservative national anchor rather than a California-style upper benchmark.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Level, momentum, one-off, and mechanism: level starts from the low-to-mid 80s for the national 65+ B28005 share. Momentum is positive because each entering 65-year-old cohort is more connected than the oldest cohorts leaving the population, and the broadband category includes cellular data plans. One-off BEAD deployment effects are limited by timing because many funded projects will not materially change 2025 ACS collection-year subscriptions. Policy support such as ACP had ended before 2025, creating some affordability drag for low-income older households.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is 2024 national 65+ broadband share estimated at 84.5 from the 2023 ACS 5-year United States B28005 share of 82.6, current all-household broadband 91.0, 65+ no-computer 8.7, and 2024 California 65+ same-table share 91.4. Historical sample uses recent same-concept annualized changes of roughly +2.3, +2.1, and +1.6 percentage points, giving sigma = sqrt((2.3^2 + 2.1^2 + 1.6^2) / 3) = 2.0 percentage points. Mechanism update adds +1.5 points for cohort replacement and continued mobile/fixed-wireless subscription diffusion, minus 0.0 to 0.3 for affordability drag, yielding point 86.0. The 80% half-width is about 1.28*sigma = 1.28*2.0 = 2.6, so bounds are 86.0 - 2.6 = 83.4 and 86.0 + 2.6 = 88.6.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected jump in cellular-data-plan reporting or older-adult smartphone adoption, which would land above the interval if the 2025 share exceeds 88.6 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest and lowest-income households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 83.4 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a definitional/table change or a large one-year ACS sampling/weighting move rather than ordinary trend continuation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":86.0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":83.4,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":88.6}]}\\\\\\\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-21/2026-07-21t01-39-25z-census-acs-broadband-subscription-65-plus-share-2025/pre_submit_review_codex_stdout.jsonl:5:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\