ill not materially change 2025 ACS collection-year subscriptions. Policy support such as ACP had ended before 2025, creating some affordability drag for low-income older households.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is 2024 national 65+ broadband share estimated at 84.5 from the 2023 ACS 5-year United States B28005 share of 82.6, current all-household broadband 91.0, 65+ no-computer 8.7, and 2024 California 65+ same-table share 91.4. Historical sample uses recent same-concept annualized changes of roughly +2.3, +2.1, and +1.6 percentage points, giving sigma = sqrt((2.3^2 + 2.1^2 + 1.6^2) / 3) = 2.0 percentage points. Mechanism update adds +1.5 points for cohort replacement and continued mobile/fixed-wireless subscription diffusion, minus 0.0 to 0.3 for affordability drag, yielding point 86.0. The 80% half-width is about 1.28*sigma = 1.28*2.0 = 2.6, so bounds are 86.0 - 2.6 = 83.4 and 86.0 + 2.6 = 88.6.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected jump in cellular-data-plan reporting or older-adult smartphone adoption, which would land above the interval if the 2025 share exceeds 88.6 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest and lowest-income households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 83.4 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a definitional/table change or a large one-year ACS sampling/weighting move rather than ordinary trend continuation.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":86.0,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":83.4,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":88.6}]}\\\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-21/2026-07-21t01-39-25z-census-acs-broadband-subscription-65-plus-share-2025/pre_submit_review_codex_stdout.jsonl:5:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The target contract is mostly coherent, but the draft relies on an estimated 2024 prior and unstated annual-change sample when exact same-series ACS 1-year history should be available.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requiredFixes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model_prior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The draft uses a constructed 2024 national prior and California/5-year proxies instead of citing the same-series U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 history available before resolution.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetch and state the latest available U.S. ACS 1-year B28005_017E/B28005_014E values, preferably a short 2021-2024 same-series history, or explicitly explain why those exact annual observations cannot be used.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"interval\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The interval sigma is based on annualized changes of +2.3, +2.1, and +1.6 percentage points, but the source observations for those changes are not shown and do not appear in historicalContext.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"List the exact historical observations used to derive those annual changes, or recompute the interval from a transparent volatility/uncertainty method tied to cited same-series or closely comparable data.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"prior_update_interval\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The compact Prior/update/interval step names a prior and sigma, but both depend on undocumented estimates, so the step is not auditable enough for publication.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Revise the step to name the actual historical sample, the persistence prior from that sample, each adjustment component, the interval method, and the resulting 80% bounds.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolver\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"warning\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The resolver text and variables are clear, but the reasoning says the 2026-09-10 release date is only expected and should be rechecked, while the ledger target treats it as the resolution date.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Either cite the official Census release-calendar evidence supporting 2026-09-10, or keep the ledger date and explicitly flag the release-date uncertainty as a ledger-calendar caveat rather than a resolved fact.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"optionalSuggestions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Add the 2024 U.S. ACS 1-year same-table value to historicalContext and demote California to an external benchmark.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Make the downside tail more concrete by quantifying what prior-year stagnation or subsidy-related churn would imply for the percentage point change.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Check that all JSON fields continue to use percent units and one-decimal rounding after revising the prior and interval.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-21/2026-07-21t01-39-25z-census-acs-broadband-subscription-65-plus-share-2025/pre_submit_review_command.j