fficial results page for latest ACS summary measures\",\n        \"result\": \"Fetched latest ACS summary measures for the United States: households with a computer 95.5 percent; households with a broadband Internet subscription 91.0 percent; population 65+ with no computer in household 8.7 percent.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"tool\",\n        \"tool\": \"official.lookup\",\n        \"call\": \"Inspect Census ACS summary-file and release-calendar pages for release mechanics\",\n        \"result\": \"Fetched Census summary-file metadata: 2024 ACS 1-year summary file table B28005 file acsdt1y2024-b28005.dat was listed with timestamp 2025-09-11 09:00 and size 1.3M; Census upcoming releases page was last revised July 20, 2026 and listed September 2026 ACS release entries including 9/10/2026 for the 2025 ACS 1-year first-release slot.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Base rate/reference class: the best reference class is the same U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 age-65-plus series, which rose from 79.4 percent in 2021 to 84.8 percent in 2024. The 2024 all-household broadband measure at 91.0 percent is an upper-adoption context, while the 8.7 percent 65+ no-computer measure constrains how fast the B28005 broadband share can close the remaining gap.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and mechanism: level starts from the 2024 same-series first-print value of 84.8 percent. Momentum is positive because each entering 65-year-old cohort is more connected than the oldest cohorts leaving the population, and the broadband category includes cellular data plans. One-off BEAD deployment effects are limited by timing because many funded projects will not materially change 2025 ACS collection-year subscriptions. ACP expiration before 2025 adds affordability drag for some low-income older households.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"math\",\n        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is the latest same-series 2024 U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 share of 84.8 percent. Historical sample is the same-series annual changes 2021-2022: 81.6 - 79.4 = +2.2, 2022-2023: 83.5 - 81.6 = +1.9, and 2023-2024: 84.8 - 83.5 = +1.3 percentage points, giving sigma = sqrt((2.2^2 + 1.9^2 + 1.3^2) / 3) = 1.8 percentage points. Mechanism update adds +1.4 points for cohort replacement and continued mobile/fixed-wireless subscription diffusion, minus 0.2 for affordability drag and saturation, yielding point 86.0. The 80% half-width is about 1.28*sigma = 1.28*1.8 = 2.3, rounded to 2.4 for first-print sampling and table-noise risk, so bounds are 86.0 - 2.4 = 83.6 and 86.0 + 2.4 = 88.4.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected jump in cellular-data-plan reporting or older-adult smartphone adoption, which would land above the interval if the 2025 share exceeds 88.4 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest and lowest-income households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 83.6 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a definitional/table change or a large one-year ACS sampling/weighting move rather than ordinary trend continuation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Review disposition: accepted the reviewer\\u2019s blocking fixes by replacing the constructed 2024 prior and undocumented change sample with same-series 2021-2024 U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 observations, recomputing sigma from those changes, and revising the interval. Kept the ledger 2026-09-10 resolution date and clarified it is supported by the official Census upcoming-releases ACS first-release slot.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"kind\": \"forecast\",\n        \"point\": 86.0,\n        \"ciLow\": 83.6,\n        \"ciHigh\": 88.4\n      }\n    ],\n    \"agentReportedRunAt\": \"2026-07-21T01:39:34Z\",\n    \"runStartedAt\": \"2026-07-21T01:39:25Z\",\n    \"promptMode\": \"fast\",\n    \"predictionDistribution\": {\n      \"format\": \"numeric_cdf_v1\",\n      \"pointCount\": 201,\n      \"support\": {\n        \"lower\": 80.0,\n        \"upper\": 92.0\n      },\n      \"points\": [\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.0,\n          \"probability\": 0.0\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.06,\n          \"probability\": 0.00166666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.12,\n          \"probability\": 0.00333333333333\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.18,\n          \"probability\": 0.005\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.24,\n          \"probability\": 0.00666666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.3,\n          \"probability\": 0.00833333333333\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.36,\n          \"probability\": 0.01\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.42,\n          \"probability\": 0.0116666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.48,\n          \"probability\": 0.0133333333333\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.54,\n          \"probability\": 0.015\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.6,\n          \"probability\": 0.0166666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.66,\n          \"probability\": 0.0183333333333\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.72,\n          \"probability\": 0.02\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.78,\n          \"probability\": 0.0216666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.84,\n          \"probability\": 0.0233333333333\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.9,\n          \"probability\": 0.025\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 80.96,\n          \"probability\": 0.0266666666667\n        },\n        {\n          \"value\": 81.02,\n          \"probability\": 0.02