band definition includes cellular data, satellite, fixed wireless, and other non-dial-up service\",\"Adoption gains should slow as the remaining offline 65+ population is more selected\",\"Affordable Connectivity Program expiration creates affordability drag for low-income older households\",\"ACS first-print sampling and weighting noise can move this national age subgroup around one percentage point\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2025.B28005?q=B28005\",\"https://api.census.gov/data/2024/acs/acs1/groups/B28005.html\",\"https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2024.B28005?g=010XX00US&q=B28005\",\"https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2023.B28005?g=010XX00US&q=B28005\",\"https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2022.B28005?g=010XX00US&q=B28005\",\"https://data.census.gov/table/ACSDT1Y2021.B28005?g=010XX00US&q=B28005\",\"https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/news/data-releases/2024/release-schedule.html\",\"https://www.census.gov/data/what-is-data-census-gov/upcoming-releases.html\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-21T02:29:30Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for 2025 ACS 65+ Broadband Subscription Share\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Framing and exact resolver: the target is Census ACS Detailed Table B28005, universe population in households, United States, 2025 ACS 1-year first print. The numerator is B28005_017E, 65 years and over, has a computer, with a broadband Internet subscription; the denominator is B28005_014E, total 65 years and over. The resolution source URL is the exact data.census.gov table page for ACSDT1Y2025.B28005.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Inspect Census API variable metadata for 2024 ACS 1-year B28005\",\"result\":\"Fetched variable definitions: B28005 has 78 variables; B28005_014E is total 65 years and over; B28005_017E is 65 years and over with a computer and a broadband Internet subscription; B28005_001E is the total table population count.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Inspect published Thesis record derived from Census data.census.gov 2021 and 2022 ACS 1-year B28005 United States rows\",\"result\":\"Fetched 2021 ACS 1-year United States B28005 counts: 65 years and over total 55,086,561 and 65+ with broadband subscription 43,757,048, computed share 79.4 percent. Fetched 2022 ACS 1-year counts: 65 years and over total 56,337,585 and 65+ with broadband subscription 45,989,221, computed share 81.6 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Inspect published Thesis record derived from Census data.census.gov 2023 and 2024 ACS 1-year B28005 United States rows\",\"result\":\"Fetched 2023 ACS 1-year United States B28005 counts: 65 years and over total 57,740,580 and 65+ with broadband subscription 48,245,700, computed share 83.5 percent. Fetched 2024 ACS 1-year counts: 65 years and over total 59,125,675 and 65+ with broadband subscription 50,144,228, computed share 84.8 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Inspect Census ACS computer-and-internet use official results summarized in the published Thesis record\",\"result\":\"Fetched latest ACS summary measures for the United States: households with a computer 95.5 percent; households with a broadband Internet subscription 91.0 percent; population 65+ with no computer in household 8.7 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Inspect Census ACS release schedule and data.census.gov upcoming releases pages\",\"result\":\"Fetched 2024 ACS release schedule showing the 2024 ACS 1-year Data Release on September 11, 2025 with Detailed Tables and Summary File for geographies of 65,000+ population. Fetched data.census.gov upcoming releases page last revised July 20, 2026 showing 9/10/2026 and 9/24/2026 September entries, but the 9/10/2026 listed item is August 2026 Current Population Survey Basic Monthly rather than ACS; this is a ledger-date discrepancy, so I keep the target's 2026-09-10 date while flagging that I did not find an official 2025 ACS 1-year schedule page verifying ACS on that date.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base rate/reference class: the best reference class is the same U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 age-65-plus series, which rose from 79.4 percent in 2021 to 84.8 percent in 2024. The 2024 all-household broadband measure at 91.0 percent is an upper-adoption context, while the 8.7 percent 65+ no-computer measure constrains how fast the B28005 broadband share can close the remaining gap.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, one-off, and mechanism: level starts from the 2024 same-series first-print value of 84.8 percent. Momentum is positive because each entering 65-year-old cohort is more connected than the oldest cohorts leaving the population, and the broadband category includes cellular data plans. One-off BEAD deployment effects are limited by timing because many funded projects will not materially change 2025 ACS collection-year subscriptions. ACP expiration before 2025 adds affordability drag for some low-income older households.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is the latest same-series 2024 U.S. ACS 1-year B28005 share of 84.8 percent. Historical sample is the same-series annual changes 2021-2022: 81.6 - 79.4 = +2.2, 2022-2023: 83.5 - 81.6 = +1.9, and 2023-2024: 84.8 - 83.5 = +1.3 percentage points, giving sigma = sqrt((2.2^2 + 1.9^2 + 1.3^2) / 3) = 1.8 percentage points. Mechanism update adds +1.4 points for cohort replacement and continued mobile/fixed-wireless subscription diffusion, minus 0.2 for affordability drag and saturation, yielding point 86.0. The 80% half-width is about 1.28*sigma = 1.28*1.8 = 2.3, rounded to 2.4 for first-print sampling and table-noise risk, so bounds are 86.0 - 2.4 = 83.6 and 86.0 + 2.4 = 88.4.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a stronger-than-expected jump in cellular-data-plan reporting or older-adult smartphone adoption, which would land above the interval if the 2025 share exceeds 88.4 percent. Downside risk is affordability churn after subsidy expiration, survey-composition noise, or a stall among the oldest and lowest-income households, which would land below the interval if the first print is under 83.6 percent. An outside the interval result would most likely reflect either a definitional/table change or a large one-year ACS sampling/weighting move rather than ordinary trend continuation.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior run update: I inspected an existing published Thesis run for the same target only for official-source-derived history and target identity, not as ground truth. Its point and interval were already consistent with the same-series 2021-2024 history, so the update from that run is no change to the central forecast and only an explicit note that the 2026-09-10 ledger date was not independently confirmed as an ACS release date on the current Census upcoming-releases page.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":86.0,\"ciLow\":83.6,\"ciHigh\":88.4}]}"}}
